960s and 1970s saw MIT\u2019s AI Lab become a hive of radical ideas. Under Minsky\u2019s leadership, the lab pursued the vision of \u201cstrong AI\u201d\u2014machines with human-like understanding\u2014through projects like SHRDLU, a natural language understanding system developed by Terry Winograd in 1970 that could manipulate blocks in a simulated world using English commands. Though limited in scope, SHRDLU demonstrated that syntax and semantics could be computationally modeled in tandem, influencing generations of NLP research. Simultaneously, researchers like Gerald Jay Sussman and Guy Steele developed Scheme, a minimalist dialect of Lisp that emphasized clarity and elegance, further cementing MIT\u2019s reputation for theoretical rigor in computer science. The lab also pioneered early work in computer vision, with David Marr\u2019s computational theory of vision\u2014though developed at MIT and later refined at the University of Utah\u2014providing a framework that still underpins modern object recognition systems.\n\nPerhaps MIT\u2019s most enduring contribution lies in its refusal to silo AI within computer science alone. The institute fostered deep integration with cognitive science, neuroscience, linguistics, and even philosophy. The founding of the MIT Cognitive Science Department in 1979 (later absorbed into Brain and Cognitive Sciences) brought together figures like Noam Chomsky, whose theories of universal grammar influenced early AI approaches to language, and Steven Pinker, whose work on language acquisition informed computational models of learning. This cross-pollination led to the development of connectionist models that sought to bridge symbolic AI with neural mechanisms\u2014a precursor to today\u2019s deep learning revolution. Even as the AI winter of the 1980s and 1990s dampened enthusiasm elsewhere, MIT researchers persisted, exploring probabilistic reasoning, Bayesian networks, and reinforcement learning\u2014areas that would later become central to modern AI.\n\nThe turn of the 21st century marked a resurgence, and MIT was again at the forefront. The establishment of the Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (CSAIL) in 2003 merged the AI Lab with the Laboratory for Computer Science, creating one of the largest and most influential AI research centers in the world. Under directors like Rodney Brooks\u2014who championed behavior-based robotics and challenged the dominance of symbolic AI with his \u201csubsumption architecture\u201d\u2014and later Daniela Rus, CSAIL expanded its scope to include autonomous systems, human-robot interaction, and AI for social good. Brooks\u2019 work on robots like Cog and Kismet, designed to learn through embodiment and social interaction, anticipated modern approaches to grounded AI and developmental robotics.\n\nMIT\u2019s influence accelerated with the rise of deep learning. While the breakthroughs in convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) were often attributed to researchers in Canada and Japan, MIT provided critical theoretical and infrastructural support. Researchers like Tomaso Poggio, who led the Center for Brains, Minds and Machines (CBMM)\u2014a multi-institutional NSF-funded initiative headquartered at MIT\u2014worked to unify theories of intelligence across biological and artificial systems. Poggio\u2019s group contributed to understanding the generalization properties of deep networks, the role of hierarchical feature learning, and the connections between deep learning and classical regularization theory. Meanwhile, MIT\u2019s Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS) became a hub for training the next generation of AI leaders, with courses like 6.867 (Machine Learning) and 6.864 (Advanced Natural Language Processing) becoming global benchmarks for rigor and relevance.\n\nThe institute\u2019s impact is also evident in its entrepreneurial ecosystem. MIT has spun out more AI-related star